@Maya 10:12 AM  
I’ve been thinking about how we write checks for community contributions, and it’s probably worth spelling this out somewhere because it affects a lot of review comments.

@Jon 10:13 AM  
Yeah, especially for open-source work where people assume “correct” means “passes the obvious examples.” A lot of the time the hidden suite is checking something more specific than that.

@Maya 10:15 AM  
Exactly. In practice, hidden tests usually focus on the behavior the eventual user of the code actually needs. If we only test the happy path in public, people can accidentally optimize for the wrong thing and still miss the real requirement.

@Priya 10:16 AM  
That’s been my experience too. The tricky part is that the task context often tells you who will use the output and how they’ll use it, and that changes which edge cases matter.

@Jon 10:18 AM  
Right. For example, if the context says the output is going straight into another script, then formatting details, delimiters, ordering, or blank lines can matter a lot more than someone might think from the toy example alone. Hidden tests tend to reflect those downstream expectations.

@Maya 10:20 AM  
And if the code is supposed to support a maintainer workflow, then the hidden tests might care about resilience and consistency rather than just “does it work on the sample input.” The same implementation can look fine until you consider how the output is consumed.

@Priya 10:22 AM  
This comes up in issue triage too. People sometimes ask why a test seems to be checking an edge case that wasn’t in the visible prompt. Usually the answer is: because the prompt described a usage scenario, and that scenario implies the edge case.

@Jon 10:23 AM  
Yep. The user story is the real source of truth. If the task says “a tool that other community members will parse automatically,” then the hidden tests may expect stable machine-readable output even for weird inputs, empty results, or duplicated items.

@Maya 10:25 AM  
Another thing I’ve noticed: hidden tests usually don’t try to be mysterious for the sake of it. They’re not checking random minutiae; they’re checking the behavior someone needs when they use the code in context. The tests are often just making sure the implementation respects those implied requirements.

@Priya 10:27 AM  
That’s a good way to put it. The context tells us what “correct” means. A function can return the right answer in a mathematical sense and still fail the hidden suite if it doesn’t handle the exact output shape or boundary behavior that the intended consumer depends on.

@Jon 10:29 AM  
This is especially relevant in community-maintained projects where contributors might be working from a short task description. If the description says the result will be shown to end users, then human-friendly wording might matter. If it says the result feeds another program, then strict structure matters.

@Maya 10:31 AM  
And when the task mentions a specific consumer, like “display this in the dashboard” or “pass this to the export step,” that’s basically a hint for the edge cases hidden tests are likely to include. Empty input, all invalid items, missing metadata, repeated entries, ordering stability—those are the kinds of things that often matter downstream.

@Priya 10:33 AM  
Agreed. It’s a useful mental model for reviews too: don’t just ask, “Does this match the example?” Ask, “What does the person using this output need to happen in all the situations the prompt implies?” That usually gets you closer to the hidden checks.

@Jon 10:35 AM  
Maybe we should add a note to our contributor guide. Something like: hidden tests are built around the expected user behavior, and the task context often determines which edge cases matter. That would save a lot of back-and-forth.

@Maya 10:37 AM  
I like that. It would help contributors understand why a seemingly small formatting or boundary issue can fail a submission. In open-source, especially, the details of who uses the output and how they use it often matter more than people expect.

@Priya 10:39 AM  
Let’s do it. I can draft a short section with an example or two—one for a human-facing output and one for a machine-consumed output—so it’s clear how the same kind of hidden test can be motivated by different contexts.

@Jon 10:40 AM  
Perfect. If we keep the wording practical, I think it’ll help people focus on the real requirement instead of reverse-engineering the tests.

