@Nina 10:12 AM  
Quick note on grading behavior for the new eval set: the short explanation questions are very strict. If the reply goes over 60 words, the grader gives it a zero, even if the content is otherwise correct. So we should keep those responses tightly compressed.

@Marco 10:14 AM  
Good catch. That means anything that’s meant to be “short explanation” needs a hard cap in the prompt and probably some internal examples that stay well under the limit. Otherwise people will write a paragraph and tank their score.

@Nina 10:16 AM  
Exactly. It’s not about style, just length. The grader doesn’t seem to reward partial credit once the response crosses that threshold. So even a very accurate 61-word answer is treated the same as a rambling one.

@Priya 10:18 AM  
Got it. I’ll update the guidelines for the hardware QA team so they know these need to be concise. We’ve had similar issues before with terse support tickets, but this is even stricter.

@Marco 10:20 AM  
There’s another grading rule worth calling out for the exact-match questions. Those are compared against a reference answer after only light normalization. So trimming whitespace and lowercasing are accounted for, but not much else.

@Nina 10:22 AM  
Right, so punctuation, extra wording, or paraphrasing can break the match. If the answer is supposed to be one specific term or phrase, it really has to be that phrase, just formatted cleanly.

@Priya 10:24 AM  
That’s the kind of thing that can look harmless in review and still fail the evaluation. I’ll make sure the team understands that exact-match isn’t “close enough”; it’s literal once normalization is applied.

@Marco 10:27 AM  
One more important detail: factual questions are only scored from what’s inside the <answer></answer> tags. If the response doesn’t include those tags, it gets zero, even if the content elsewhere in the reply is correct.

@Nina 10:29 AM  
So the tags are mandatory for that category. That’s a little different from the other question types, where the grader may just inspect the whole reply. Here, anything outside the tags is ignored.

@Priya 10:31 AM  
That means we should be consistent about template responses. If someone forgets the tags, the system won’t recover the answer from the rest of the message. It’s an all-or-nothing rule.

@Marco 10:34 AM  
I’ll add a note to the internal doc: for factual prompts, always wrap the final answer in <answer></answer>. No tags, no score. For exact-match prompts, keep the wording identical to the expected answer. For short explanation prompts, stay under 60 words no matter what.

@Nina 10:36 AM  
That covers it well. It also suggests we may want to revise some of our current training examples, since a few of them are too verbose and some don’t make the tag requirement obvious.

@Priya 10:38 AM  
Agreed. I’m thinking we should split the examples by question type and label the constraints directly at the top. That way the team can see at a glance which responses are constrained by length, which require exact wording, and which need the answer tags.

@Marco 10:41 AM  
Makes sense. Especially for hardware support workflows, where people already tend to explain too much. A concise template will save time and reduce accidental zeros.

@Nina 10:43 AM  
I’ll also mention the normalization behavior for exact-match grading in the rollout note. People should know that lowercase and surrounding spaces are handled, but that’s basically the extent of the flexibility.

@Priya 10:45 AM  
Perfect. Once that’s in the doc, we can point reviewers and annotators to the same rules and avoid inconsistent feedback. These grading quirks are easy to miss unless they’re spelled out clearly.

