@anya  
Quick note on the RL training environments: the environments themselves are written by researchers, and the grader for each one basically reflects what that researcher decided to check. In practice that means the evaluator is only as complete as the environment design, so it may not cover every detail mentioned in the original task prompt.

@miguel  
Right, that’s worth emphasizing. People sometimes assume “the prompt says X, so the model is explicitly optimized for every aspect of X,” but that isn’t always true. The grader usually encodes a subset of the task requirements — often the parts that are easiest to make objective or reliably checkable.

@anya  
Exactly. For example, if a prompt asks for a polished answer with a bunch of constraints, the environment might only verify a few of them. Or if it’s a coding task, the grader may focus on tests passing rather than style, explanation quality, or edge-case discussion unless those were explicitly built in.

@leah  
This is also why the environment mix matters so much. We’re not training on one narrow category of task; the large mixtures include coding problems, maths and logic puzzles, formatting and extraction tasks, writing tasks judged against rubrics, and multi-step agentic tasks.

@miguel  
Yep. Each of those has a different kind of signal. Coding problems are often fairly crisp because you can run tests. Maths and logic puzzles may have more exact answers but can still be graded in different ways. Formatting and extraction tasks are usually about strict adherence to structure. Writing tasks are more subjective and tend to rely on rubrics. And agentic tasks are a different beast entirely because the model has to do several steps in sequence and the environment has to decide what counts as success along the way.

@anya  
The important operational point is that the grader defines the training signal, not just the natural-language prompt. So if a researcher omits something from the grader, the model may never get rewarded or penalized for it, even if the prompt text mentioned it.

@leah  
Which is totally normal in practice. These environments are designed by humans with specific evaluation goals, and there’s always some tradeoff between completeness and making the task machine-checkable.

@miguel  
I think that nuance helps explain a lot of behavior people find surprising. A model can look very good on an environment while still missing parts of the “full” task as a human would read it, because the signal it was trained on was narrower than the prompt language suggested.

@anya  
Agreed. Worth keeping in mind anytime we talk about RL training data or benchmark-like environments: the environment author’s grader is the real specification the model sees.

