    {
      "speaker": "Jia Chen",
      "text": "And that’s where tooling matters. We’ve started linting outputs before submission, using small scripts that confirm the response is exactly one parseable JSON object and nothing else. That simple safeguard has saved us a lot of debugging time. It also aligns the lab’s workflow with the grader’s behavior, which is essential if you want the measured score to reflect actual performance rather than formatting accidents."
    },
    {
      "speaker": "Dr. Elena Morris",
      "text": "So if there’s one takeaway from today’s discussion, it’s that format compliance is not peripheral to information extraction; it is central to evaluation. In our setting, the grader’s rule is absolute: the whole reply must parse as a JSON object, or the score is zero. Understanding that constraint helps us design better systems, better prompts, and better experiments."
